+# Billing service
+
+`BillingService` (`src/service.py`) is the tenant-scoped service layer for
+billing reads and writes. `src/repo.py` holds an in-memory stand-in for the
+billing database, and `src/cache.py` holds the in-process cache.
+
+Run the tests with:
+
+```sh
+pytest
+```
+
+## Caching
+
+`BillingService` keeps some read results in one in-process `Cache`. All
+requests and all tenants handled by that service instance share it. Writes
+always go straight to the database.
+
+### Which reads are cached
+
+| Read                  | Cached? | Notes |
+|-----------------------|---------|-------|
+| `get_customer`        | Yes     | Keyed by customer id. |
+| `get_invoice_summary` | Yes     | Keyed by period. |
+| `get_total_paid`      | Yes     | Keyed by customer id. |
+| `get_balance`         | No      | Always queries the DB and clears the whole cache (see below). |
+| `list_open_invoices`  | No      | Always queries the DB and clears the whole cache (see below). |
+
+**The `internal` tenant is never cached.** Every read for tenant `internal`
+goes to the database. The audit service regenerates that data on every
+request, and serving stale values would be a compliance issue (SOX control
+C-14).
+
+**`get_balance` and `list_open_invoices` are never cached.** This comes from
+an old workaround. Users were sometimes seeing other tenants' data, and
+before the cause was known these two reads were changed to skip the cache
+and clear the entire cache on every call. BILL-2291 later found the cause:
+cache keys did not include the tenant id. Keys now include it (see
+[Tenant isolation](#tenant-isolation)), so this special case is probably
+no longer needed. It is still in the code. Two things follow from it:
+
+- Balances and open-invoice lists always reflect the database at the moment
+  of the request.
+- Every call to these two reads (other than for tenant `internal`) empties
+  the cache for **all** tenants, so the next cached read for anyone goes to
+  the database.
+
+### What invalidates cached data
+
+1. **Writes through the service.** `upsert_customer`, `create_invoice` and
+   `mark_invoice_paid` each clear the entire cache, for every tenant, after
+   the database write. The next read picks up the change straight away.
+2. **Time-to-live (TTL) expiry.** Each entry expires `ttl_seconds` after it
+   was loaded (default: 300 seconds, set by `DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS`). The
+   service checks expiry when the entry is read. An expired entry is thrown
+   away and reloaded from the database, and the reloaded entry starts a new
+   TTL.
+3. **`get_balance` / `list_open_invoices` calls**, which clear the entire
+   cache as a side effect (see above). Do not rely on this: it is a leftover
+   workaround and may be removed.
+
+Nothing else invalidates cached data. In particular, **changes made
+directly in the database are not detected.** For example, back-office tools
+that edit some tenants' data without going through the service
+(BILL-2324) are only picked up when the TTL expires.
+
+### How stale data can be
+
+| How the data changed | When customers see it |
+|---|---|
+| Write through the same `BillingService` instance | On the next read (immediately). |
+| Direct DB edit (back-office tools, scripts, another process) | Within `ttl_seconds` (default up to **5 minutes**). |
+| Any change, for tenant `internal` | Immediately (never cached). |
+| Any change, for `get_balance` / `list_open_invoices` | Immediately (never cached). |
+
+Details:
+
+- The TTL clock starts just **before** the database load, so the load time
+  counts toward the TTL. A cached value is never served more than
+  `ttl_seconds` after the load that produced it began.
+- Time is measured with `time.monotonic` by default, so wall-clock changes do
+  not affect expiry. You can inject a different clock through the `clock`
+  constructor argument (the tests do this).
+- `ttl_seconds=0` turns caching off entirely. Negative values are rejected.
+- The TTL applies to each entry separately. Entries loaded at different times
+  expire at different times, so one page can show a fresh customer record
+  next to an invoice summary that is almost `ttl_seconds` old.
+- Invalidation only clears the `Cache` object that the service instance
+  doing the write is using. If several processes or service instances each
+  have their own cache, a write through one of them does not clear the
+  others. Those instances behave as if the data had been edited directly in
+  the database: stale for up to `ttl_seconds`.
+
+### Tenant isolation
+
+The single cache is shared across tenants, so every key includes the tenant.
+`cache.make_key` builds keys as the tuple `(tenant_id, op, entity_id)`. For
+example, customer `c-1` of tenant `acme` and customer `c-1` of tenant
+`globex` are stored under different keys and can never be served to each
+other. Keys are tuples, not joined strings, so ids that contain separator
+characters cannot collide.
+
+Before BILL-2291, keys did not include the tenant id, which caused the
+cross-tenant leak described above. The tests
+`test_summary_not_shared_between_tenants_without_writes`,
+`test_customer_not_shared_between_tenants` and the other `*_isolated_*` /
+`*_not_shared_*` tests in `tests/test_service.py` guard against it coming
+back.
+
+Note that invalidation is **not** tenant-scoped. A write for one tenant
+clears cached entries for every tenant. This costs extra DB reads for
+tenants that did not change, but it never makes data more stale.
